00:00:28.070 --> 00:00:33.520
My name is Joe Kava, and I'm the
vice president of data centers.

00:00:33.520 --> 00:00:35.870
So that means that I lead
the teams the design,

00:00:35.870 --> 00:00:40.040
build, and operate our
data centers globally.

00:00:40.040 --> 00:00:43.160
And I really appreciate your
interest in this topic today.

00:00:43.160 --> 00:00:46.100
So I'm going to share with you
a little bit about what makes

00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:47.495
Google data centers so unique.

00:00:50.990 --> 00:00:53.870
So let's first start by
considering what do you

00:00:53.870 --> 00:00:55.060
need from your data center.

00:01:03.621 --> 00:01:04.120
Right.

00:01:04.120 --> 00:01:06.280
Sorry, we're going
to go to plan B. See,

00:01:06.280 --> 00:01:08.710
as a good data center guy,
I've always got a backup plan.

00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:14.220
Anyhow, so whether
you're currently

00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:16.710
in your own
enterprise data center

00:01:16.710 --> 00:01:20.160
or you're in a multi-tenant
colocation facility, what

00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:24.990
you need is access to high
performance computing,

00:01:24.990 --> 00:01:28.830
always available around the
world, when you need it,

00:01:28.830 --> 00:01:31.040
where you need it.

00:01:31.040 --> 00:01:33.530
That's exactly what you get.

00:01:40.170 --> 00:01:44.460
I know that-- as I
mentioned, you need access

00:01:44.460 --> 00:01:47.340
to high performance computing
available around the world,

00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:51.800
in highly available and
secure data centers.

00:01:51.800 --> 00:01:55.420
Well, with Google Cloud
that's exactly what you get.

00:01:55.420 --> 00:01:58.610
You get it where, when,
and how you need it.

00:02:01.370 --> 00:02:04.550
We've been leaders in developing
ultra-high performance reliable

00:02:04.550 --> 00:02:09.000
compute systems since the
earliest days at Google

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:11.430
and using the most
innovative designs.

00:02:11.430 --> 00:02:15.480
And no matter how good our
teams and our systems perform,

00:02:15.480 --> 00:02:17.500
we always feel that
we can do better.

00:02:17.500 --> 00:02:19.960
So we have a relentless
focus on innovation.

00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:25.460
And our high
performance computing

00:02:25.460 --> 00:02:29.900
is the result of optimizing
throughout the entire stack.

00:02:29.900 --> 00:02:32.090
Nearly all of our
infrastructure is

00:02:32.090 --> 00:02:34.550
custom designed
and purpose-built

00:02:34.550 --> 00:02:39.100
for our own computing needs,
from our own dedicated

00:02:39.100 --> 00:02:42.970
high-voltage substations,
to our customized electrical

00:02:42.970 --> 00:02:46.570
distribution systems, to
our cooling systems designed

00:02:46.570 --> 00:02:50.480
and built purposely just
for our data centers,

00:02:50.480 --> 00:02:53.710
all working in
conjunction and optimized

00:02:53.710 --> 00:02:56.030
to provide the highest
performance and lowest

00:02:56.030 --> 00:03:00.260
total cost of ownership
computing anywhere.

00:03:05.540 --> 00:03:08.480
You know, when I first
joined Google nine years ago,

00:03:08.480 --> 00:03:10.760
I was amazed at the
extraordinary lengths

00:03:10.760 --> 00:03:15.590
that the company went to
build our own systems.

00:03:15.590 --> 00:03:17.210
And of course, as
I learned more,

00:03:17.210 --> 00:03:18.930
it became obvious
why we do that.

00:03:18.930 --> 00:03:20.490
But at the time I
thought to myself--

00:03:20.490 --> 00:03:22.430
I'm like, what
makes us so unique,

00:03:22.430 --> 00:03:25.520
why don't we just buy
servers like everyone else?

00:03:25.520 --> 00:03:29.240
Well, we go through all
the extraordinary effort

00:03:29.240 --> 00:03:33.020
because, frankly, what
we needed at our scale

00:03:33.020 --> 00:03:35.360
didn't exist when we started.

00:03:35.360 --> 00:03:38.990
In order to achieve the
performance, efficiency

00:03:38.990 --> 00:03:43.560
and price targets, we had
to build our own servers.

00:03:43.560 --> 00:03:47.900
We had to develop and create
the hardware, and the software,

00:03:47.900 --> 00:03:52.180
and the culture of reliability
to make Google successful.

00:03:55.020 --> 00:03:58.350
Our servers don't include
any unnecessary components,

00:03:58.350 --> 00:04:02.430
such as video cards, chipsets
or peripheral connectors,

00:04:02.430 --> 00:04:05.680
which can introduce
vulnerabilities.

00:04:05.680 --> 00:04:08.560
Our production servers
run a custom designed

00:04:08.560 --> 00:04:13.890
and stripped-down version of
Linux that's been hardened,

00:04:13.890 --> 00:04:15.670
and our servers
and their OSes are

00:04:15.670 --> 00:04:21.810
designed for the sole purpose of
powering Google services only.

00:04:21.810 --> 00:04:25.670
Our server resources are
provisioned dynamically.

00:04:25.670 --> 00:04:31.260
They are allocated and allow
for us to be flexible in growth.

00:04:31.260 --> 00:04:34.650
And they can be reprovisioned
quickly and efficiently,

00:04:34.650 --> 00:04:38.430
adding capacity where it's
needed, when it's needed,

00:04:38.430 --> 00:04:41.250
to quickly respond
to customer demand.

00:04:43.850 --> 00:04:46.700
And recently we've
also contributed

00:04:46.700 --> 00:04:50.780
our learning and our experience
with our custom 48 volt rack

00:04:50.780 --> 00:04:51.800
systems.

00:04:51.800 --> 00:04:55.370
We've contributed that design
to the Open Compute program,

00:04:55.370 --> 00:04:58.650
so now Open Compute
program racks also work

00:04:58.650 --> 00:04:59.780
in Google data centers.

00:05:07.460 --> 00:05:09.770
And you know, since
I've been at Google,

00:05:09.770 --> 00:05:12.380
we've fundamentally
redesigned our cooling systems

00:05:12.380 --> 00:05:15.230
about every 12 to 18 months.

00:05:15.230 --> 00:05:17.840
And along the way we've
pioneered and developed

00:05:17.840 --> 00:05:21.530
advancements in water-based
cooling systems,

00:05:21.530 --> 00:05:26.480
such as seawater cooling,
recycled gray water cooling,

00:05:26.480 --> 00:05:30.920
storm water capture and
reuse, rainwater harvesting,

00:05:30.920 --> 00:05:36.610
industrial canal water use
and thermal energy storage.

00:05:36.610 --> 00:05:38.110
And we've also
designed data centers

00:05:38.110 --> 00:05:40.880
that don't require any water
for the cooling at all.

00:05:40.880 --> 00:05:44.860
Instead, they're cooled with
a 100% outside fresh air.

00:05:44.860 --> 00:05:49.360
The point is that there is no
one-size-fits-all model here.

00:05:49.360 --> 00:05:51.190
All of our data
center designs are

00:05:51.190 --> 00:05:55.630
custom for that specific
region, that specific location,

00:05:55.630 --> 00:05:57.050
to get the best efficiency.

00:06:00.310 --> 00:06:05.250
We also invest a lot in robotic
innovation in our data centers.

00:06:05.250 --> 00:06:08.280
Each of our data centers has
fully automated disk erase

00:06:08.280 --> 00:06:09.690
environments.

00:06:09.690 --> 00:06:13.290
This allows for faster,
higher throughput,

00:06:13.290 --> 00:06:16.718
more efficient and better
inventory management.

00:06:21.400 --> 00:06:24.370
Now, of course, along with
performance and innovation

00:06:24.370 --> 00:06:28.030
you need data centers in
locations that work for you.

00:06:28.030 --> 00:06:31.300
And at Google, our numerous
locations and fiber network

00:06:31.300 --> 00:06:33.070
differentiates us from the rest.

00:06:37.890 --> 00:06:43.260
Our IP network consists of
our own fiber, public fiber

00:06:43.260 --> 00:06:44.910
and undersea cables.

00:06:44.910 --> 00:06:48.630
And this allows us to deliver
highly available and low

00:06:48.630 --> 00:06:51.840
latency services
across the globe.

00:06:51.840 --> 00:06:55.680
But it also means that we
can respond faster to demand,

00:06:55.680 --> 00:06:59.460
efficiently scaling our
capacity for when you need it

00:06:59.460 --> 00:07:01.140
and where you need it.

00:07:01.140 --> 00:07:04.710
It's even been noted by
several industry publications

00:07:04.710 --> 00:07:09.150
that this is clearly
a standout for Google.

00:07:09.150 --> 00:07:11.180
And of course, as
you can imagine

00:07:11.180 --> 00:07:14.490
with the explosive growth
in public cloud computing,

00:07:14.490 --> 00:07:19.640
we've had some pretty aggressive
expansion going on ourselves.

00:07:19.640 --> 00:07:21.200
And of course,
our global network

00:07:21.200 --> 00:07:26.350
brings our data centers close
together and closer to you.

00:07:26.350 --> 00:07:30.040
We're available currently
in six regions and 18 zones

00:07:30.040 --> 00:07:31.915
with over a 100
points of presence.

00:07:34.650 --> 00:07:37.140
And our data
network is comprised

00:07:37.140 --> 00:07:40.160
of hundreds of thousands of
miles of fiber optic cable.

00:07:42.800 --> 00:07:46.280
To ensure every customer has
a cloud region near them,

00:07:46.280 --> 00:07:50.007
Google Cloud will add
several more regions in 2017,

00:07:50.007 --> 00:07:51.590
including the ones
that were announced

00:07:51.590 --> 00:07:53.450
just this morning by orders.

00:07:53.450 --> 00:07:56.210
So we now have new regions
available in the Netherlands,

00:07:56.210 --> 00:08:00.200
Canada and California.

00:08:00.200 --> 00:08:03.940
We need to operate our own
data centers around the world

00:08:03.940 --> 00:08:08.280
to keep our own
products running 24x7.

00:08:08.280 --> 00:08:11.580
Our data centers are part of a
very robust infrastructure that

00:08:11.580 --> 00:08:16.740
supports Google scale and
Google's global network,

00:08:16.740 --> 00:08:20.000
including our four
owned undersea cables,

00:08:20.000 --> 00:08:24.520
just far more than any
other cloud provider has,

00:08:24.520 --> 00:08:26.200
and our Edge points
of presence where

00:08:26.200 --> 00:08:28.615
we connect Google's network
to the rest of the internet.

00:08:31.180 --> 00:08:33.289
You know, we're
peering, of course.

00:08:33.289 --> 00:08:36.110
And having those multiple points
of presence across the globe

00:08:36.110 --> 00:08:38.630
provides strong
redundancy, so your data

00:08:38.630 --> 00:08:41.360
is securely mirrored
across storage devices

00:08:41.360 --> 00:08:42.559
in multiple locations.

00:08:45.480 --> 00:08:47.480
By choosing Google
Cloud you can build

00:08:47.480 --> 00:08:49.640
on the same future-proof
infrastructure

00:08:49.640 --> 00:08:54.030
that Google builds on, the same
infrastructure that Google uses

00:08:54.030 --> 00:08:55.980
to return billions
of search results

00:08:55.980 --> 00:08:58.620
in milliseconds, the
same infrastructure

00:08:58.620 --> 00:09:06.630
that Google uses to serve over 1
billion hours of YouTube a day,

00:09:06.630 --> 00:09:09.840
the same infrastructure
that Google serves over 1

00:09:09.840 --> 00:09:11.790
billion Gmail users every day.

00:09:16.330 --> 00:09:18.730
And at the core of
our program really

00:09:18.730 --> 00:09:21.340
is our commitment
to availability.

00:09:21.340 --> 00:09:24.010
You know I've been doing
this a very long time,

00:09:24.010 --> 00:09:27.550
and I've evaluated
dozens, if not hundreds,

00:09:27.550 --> 00:09:31.070
of public and private data
centers all over the world.

00:09:31.070 --> 00:09:33.415
And I can tell you that they're
not all created equally.

00:09:38.060 --> 00:09:44.520
But when we say, it's really
7x24, we really mean it.

00:09:44.520 --> 00:09:48.750
Our designs are fault-tolerant,
and this protects you

00:09:48.750 --> 00:09:50.590
against potential failures.

00:09:50.590 --> 00:09:53.790
And our critical infrastructure
is concurrently maintainable.

00:09:53.790 --> 00:09:57.390
This means that whenever
something breaks or maintenance

00:09:57.390 --> 00:10:01.830
is needed, we don't have to
take down services to do that.

00:10:01.830 --> 00:10:03.840
There's no such thing
as scheduled outages

00:10:03.840 --> 00:10:05.880
on Google Cloud.

00:10:05.880 --> 00:10:09.270
This is invisible to
you and it enables

00:10:09.270 --> 00:10:12.000
us to do all of this live.

00:10:14.950 --> 00:10:19.100
We design our data centers
with an eye towards the future.

00:10:19.100 --> 00:10:20.590
So we build in
"hooks" that allow

00:10:20.590 --> 00:10:23.080
us to take advantage
and upgrade our systems

00:10:23.080 --> 00:10:27.720
and network with the latest
platforms developments.

00:10:27.720 --> 00:10:30.580
But we also staff
our data centers 24/7

00:10:30.580 --> 00:10:32.890
with our own team
of Googlers who

00:10:32.890 --> 00:10:35.860
have been intimately
designed from design--

00:10:35.860 --> 00:10:38.770
or they've been intimately
involved from the design,

00:10:38.770 --> 00:10:40.750
through construction
and commissioning,

00:10:40.750 --> 00:10:42.764
and through operations.

00:10:42.764 --> 00:10:44.180
They're the best
and the brightest

00:10:44.180 --> 00:10:46.040
engineers and
operations professionals

00:10:46.040 --> 00:10:48.110
available anywhere.

00:10:48.110 --> 00:10:50.540
But they all come from
very diverse backgrounds

00:10:50.540 --> 00:10:53.390
and they have one common
trait, all of them--

00:10:53.390 --> 00:10:55.840
they're systems level thinkers.

00:10:55.840 --> 00:11:00.640
Many of them have come from
mission critical environments,

00:11:00.640 --> 00:11:04.160
like the Navy nuclear
submarine program, where

00:11:04.160 --> 00:11:06.410
mistakes can be catastrophic.

00:11:06.410 --> 00:11:11.120
They understand mission
critical operations.

00:11:11.120 --> 00:11:14.780
Further, we've built site teams
at all of our data centers

00:11:14.780 --> 00:11:16.430
that are comprised
of the engineers

00:11:16.430 --> 00:11:18.560
responsible for the
design and construction

00:11:18.560 --> 00:11:20.930
of the data center,
working side by side

00:11:20.930 --> 00:11:23.430
with the operations teams.

00:11:23.430 --> 00:11:27.010
Their experience and
qualifications is unmatched.

00:11:27.010 --> 00:11:29.220
The majority of our staff
hold advanced degrees

00:11:29.220 --> 00:11:32.022
in professional licensing.

00:11:32.022 --> 00:11:34.230
They've been published in
technical papers and books,

00:11:34.230 --> 00:11:37.320
and they hold patents
relevant to the industry.

00:11:37.320 --> 00:11:39.240
And together these
integrated teams

00:11:39.240 --> 00:11:43.440
are responsible for the capacity
commissioning and providing

00:11:43.440 --> 00:11:45.540
7x24 operations.

00:11:45.540 --> 00:11:48.270
This really gives us
an unparalleled level

00:11:48.270 --> 00:11:53.290
of end-to-end ownership
of our infrastructure.

00:11:53.290 --> 00:11:57.400
Having Googlers operate our
own data centers is really key.

00:11:57.400 --> 00:11:58.870
You see, the norm
in the industry

00:11:58.870 --> 00:12:01.210
is for the
design-build contractor

00:12:01.210 --> 00:12:05.170
to hand over a set of
documents, drawings and manuals,

00:12:05.170 --> 00:12:07.990
and the keys to the front
door, and wish the data center

00:12:07.990 --> 00:12:11.340
operator good luck.

00:12:11.340 --> 00:12:14.340
And all too often
the operations teams

00:12:14.340 --> 00:12:16.740
aren't even employed by the
owner of that data center,

00:12:16.740 --> 00:12:20.400
they're actually employed by the
lowest cost outsource bidder.

00:12:20.400 --> 00:12:24.122
So the level of ownership
is maybe not quite

00:12:24.122 --> 00:12:25.330
what we believe it should be.

00:12:29.140 --> 00:12:32.630
And if there's one certainty
in mission critical operations,

00:12:32.630 --> 00:12:34.730
it's that faults
and errors are going

00:12:34.730 --> 00:12:37.850
to happen in the
middle of the night,

00:12:37.850 --> 00:12:41.360
typically on a weekend, when
no one else is around to help

00:12:41.360 --> 00:12:43.460
and all your
suppliers are closed.

00:12:43.460 --> 00:12:45.980
But that's not the case at
Google, because it's us,

00:12:45.980 --> 00:12:47.720
we're there 24/7.

00:12:47.720 --> 00:12:50.510
All of our teams are
working around the clock

00:12:50.510 --> 00:12:55.750
to provide the most available
compute infrastructure.

00:12:55.750 --> 00:12:57.310
But you know,
equally as important

00:12:57.310 --> 00:13:02.800
as the operational
excellence is transparency.

00:13:02.800 --> 00:13:06.370
And really we want to have
the continual learning,

00:13:06.370 --> 00:13:11.710
that when an issue does arise,
that we can expose that issue

00:13:11.710 --> 00:13:14.340
and sent it around the
world to all of our teams

00:13:14.340 --> 00:13:16.150
so that everyone
can learn from it.

00:13:19.130 --> 00:13:23.330
We studied the root causes
of errors and issues

00:13:23.330 --> 00:13:25.870
in the industry based
on published data.

00:13:25.870 --> 00:13:28.580
And on the infrastructure
side, the industry norm

00:13:28.580 --> 00:13:32.990
is that human error accounts
for the overwhelming majority

00:13:32.990 --> 00:13:35.220
of all incidents.

00:13:35.220 --> 00:13:37.860
However, because of our
designs and, frankly, because

00:13:37.860 --> 00:13:40.470
of our very highly
qualified people,

00:13:40.470 --> 00:13:42.870
only a small fraction
of our issues

00:13:42.870 --> 00:13:46.090
are related to human errors.

00:13:46.090 --> 00:13:49.750
And of those, none of them
has ever caused any downtime

00:13:49.750 --> 00:13:50.640
at our data centers.

00:13:55.800 --> 00:13:59.850
So in addition to high
performance, innovation

00:13:59.850 --> 00:14:02.340
and availability, you
want your data center

00:14:02.340 --> 00:14:06.750
to be environmentally
sustainable as well as secure.

00:14:06.750 --> 00:14:10.860
So first let me talk about
sustainability a little bit.

00:14:10.860 --> 00:14:14.220
Google data centers are the most
efficient and environmentally

00:14:14.220 --> 00:14:15.390
friendly in the world.

00:14:21.594 --> 00:14:23.760
For as long as I've been
building and operating data

00:14:23.760 --> 00:14:28.290
centers, I've been an
advocate for efficiency

00:14:28.290 --> 00:14:31.040
and sustainability.

00:14:31.040 --> 00:14:33.530
Running our business in
environmentally responsible way

00:14:33.530 --> 00:14:36.260
is important to us, and
it's something that we also

00:14:36.260 --> 00:14:38.711
know it's important to you.

00:14:38.711 --> 00:14:42.860
It takes a tremendous amount
of power to run at our scale.

00:14:42.860 --> 00:14:45.950
As a matter of fact, last
year, the total amount

00:14:45.950 --> 00:14:48.740
of energy consumed in
Google worldwide for all

00:14:48.740 --> 00:14:52.880
of our operations was
just almost equivalent

00:14:52.880 --> 00:14:54.530
to the total amount
of energy consumed

00:14:54.530 --> 00:14:56.700
by the city of San
Francisco in the same year.

00:14:59.210 --> 00:15:02.480
But we want to be really good
stewards to the environment,

00:15:02.480 --> 00:15:05.250
even though we are
consuming a lot of power.

00:15:05.250 --> 00:15:07.880
We want to use renewable
energy as much as possible.

00:15:10.680 --> 00:15:13.260
So this year we're
very, very excited

00:15:13.260 --> 00:15:16.590
to announce that
in 2017 Google is

00:15:16.590 --> 00:15:19.530
going to reach a 100%
renewable energy for all

00:15:19.530 --> 00:15:24.690
of our global operations, data
centers and offices combined.

00:15:24.690 --> 00:15:28.200
This means that for the
calendar year in 2017 Google

00:15:28.200 --> 00:15:31.890
is directly purchasing enough
wind and solar electricity

00:15:31.890 --> 00:15:34.440
to account for every
unit of electricity

00:15:34.440 --> 00:15:36.270
we consume globally.

00:15:36.270 --> 00:15:39.120
This is a huge milestone
for us, and it's, frankly,

00:15:39.120 --> 00:15:41.700
the culmination of
seven years of effort

00:15:41.700 --> 00:15:45.270
from all of my colleagues
across the company.

00:15:45.270 --> 00:15:48.900
So how did we get here?

00:15:48.900 --> 00:15:51.360
We started this
journey back in 2010

00:15:51.360 --> 00:15:55.740
when we did our first wind
deal with a wind farm in Iowa.

00:15:55.740 --> 00:15:58.560
Back then we were amongst
the first corporations

00:15:58.560 --> 00:16:02.550
to go out and buy
renewable energy directly.

00:16:02.550 --> 00:16:05.760
We then started expanding,
doing more deals globally

00:16:05.760 --> 00:16:08.610
and moving more into
solar along the way also.

00:16:08.610 --> 00:16:12.030
We now have 20 deals that
are equivalent to 2.6

00:16:12.030 --> 00:16:14.580
gigawatts of renewable energy.

00:16:14.580 --> 00:16:16.920
And this has generated
emissions reductions

00:16:16.920 --> 00:16:20.630
that are equivalent to taking
1.2 million cars off the road.

00:16:24.840 --> 00:16:29.910
And in doing so this makes us
the largest corporate buyer

00:16:29.910 --> 00:16:35.580
of renewable energy in the
world, and by a wide margin.

00:16:35.580 --> 00:16:39.480
And thanks in part to the work
of renewable energy tariffs,

00:16:39.480 --> 00:16:41.700
and our power
purchase agreements,

00:16:41.700 --> 00:16:45.060
and the related efforts by
our peers in the industry,

00:16:45.060 --> 00:16:47.310
the number of corporate
renewable energy deals

00:16:47.310 --> 00:16:47.880
is soaring.

00:16:50.810 --> 00:16:53.540
We've been committed
to sustainability

00:16:53.540 --> 00:16:56.240
since the earliest days of
our data center program,

00:16:56.240 --> 00:16:59.360
and we've been carbon neutral
for more than 10 years now.

00:16:59.360 --> 00:17:03.920
And in addition, we had a number
of accolades along the way,

00:17:03.920 --> 00:17:05.359
awards and high marks.

00:17:05.359 --> 00:17:08.600
Most recently, Google
achieved the final grade of A

00:17:08.600 --> 00:17:11.300
in Greenpeace's
2017 Clicking Clean

00:17:11.300 --> 00:17:15.740
report which benchmarks the
energy performance of the IT

00:17:15.740 --> 00:17:17.329
sector.

00:17:17.329 --> 00:17:20.599
And only three companies out
of the 15 that they evaluated

00:17:20.599 --> 00:17:21.857
achieved that A grade.

00:17:21.857 --> 00:17:23.440
So it's something
we're very proud of.

00:17:26.210 --> 00:17:29.950
We're very excited by the
growth in renewable energy,

00:17:29.950 --> 00:17:32.920
and we're dedicated
to increasing

00:17:32.920 --> 00:17:36.850
the ability of customers to take
control of their energy supply.

00:17:36.850 --> 00:17:38.900
But there is more
work to be done.

00:17:38.900 --> 00:17:43.420
We continue to look for new
ways to help utility business

00:17:43.420 --> 00:17:46.240
models become more
customer-centric,

00:17:46.240 --> 00:17:48.340
so that cost effective
renewable energy

00:17:48.340 --> 00:17:50.170
is available to
anyone who wants it.

00:17:53.860 --> 00:17:59.290
And finally, security is
of the utmost importance

00:17:59.290 --> 00:18:00.760
in our data centers.

00:18:00.760 --> 00:18:05.230
Our infrastructure is protected
by more than 800 top experts

00:18:05.230 --> 00:18:10.460
in information, application
and network security.

00:18:10.460 --> 00:18:13.620
And Google's focus on
security and data protection

00:18:13.620 --> 00:18:17.240
is amongst our primary
design criteria.

00:18:17.240 --> 00:18:21.170
Our physical security team or
our physical security systems

00:18:21.170 --> 00:18:23.630
feature a layered
security model,

00:18:23.630 --> 00:18:26.930
including safeguards like custom
designed electronic access

00:18:26.930 --> 00:18:32.060
cards, alarms, vehicle access
barriers, perimeter fencing,

00:18:32.060 --> 00:18:36.360
metal detectors,
biometric authentication.

00:18:36.360 --> 00:18:39.980
And our data center floors
are protected by a laser beam

00:18:39.980 --> 00:18:43.480
intrusion detection.

00:18:43.480 --> 00:18:46.060
Our data centers
are monitored 24/7

00:18:46.060 --> 00:18:49.180
by high resolution interior
and exterior cameras that

00:18:49.180 --> 00:18:52.270
can detect and track intruders.

00:18:52.270 --> 00:18:57.200
Access logs, activity
records and camera footage

00:18:57.200 --> 00:19:00.940
are available in case
any incident does occur.

00:19:00.940 --> 00:19:03.560
And our data centers are
also routinely patrolled

00:19:03.560 --> 00:19:05.510
by very experienced
security guards

00:19:05.510 --> 00:19:10.130
who have undergone rigorous
background checks and training.

00:19:10.130 --> 00:19:12.770
And you know, the closer you
get to the data center floor,

00:19:12.770 --> 00:19:16.906
the more rigorous the
security systems get also.

00:19:16.906 --> 00:19:19.800
As a matter of fact, to gain
access to the data center

00:19:19.800 --> 00:19:23.070
floor, it's only available
through a secure corridor that

00:19:23.070 --> 00:19:27.510
requires badge access, biometric
authentication and passing

00:19:27.510 --> 00:19:30.700
through anti-tailgate devices.

00:19:30.700 --> 00:19:33.640
And only approved
employees that have

00:19:33.640 --> 00:19:37.390
a specific role to beyond
the center floor ever set

00:19:37.390 --> 00:19:38.110
foot there.

00:19:38.110 --> 00:19:41.080
That means that less than 1%
of our employee population

00:19:41.080 --> 00:19:45.370
ever set foot in one of
our own data centers.

00:19:45.370 --> 00:19:49.050
We employ a very
strict end-to-end chain

00:19:49.050 --> 00:19:52.770
of physical custody for storage,
tracking everything from cradle

00:19:52.770 --> 00:19:57.300
to grave, from the first time
a hard disk goes into a machine

00:19:57.300 --> 00:20:00.100
until it's verified,
cleaned and erased.

00:20:00.100 --> 00:20:02.820
We know everything
about that media--

00:20:02.820 --> 00:20:06.570
we know who touches it,
when it was touched,

00:20:06.570 --> 00:20:09.840
what it was touched
for and why, what's

00:20:09.840 --> 00:20:11.760
its next expected action.

00:20:15.730 --> 00:20:17.900
Our information security
and physical security

00:20:17.900 --> 00:20:19.400
go hand in hand.

00:20:19.400 --> 00:20:21.320
As you know, data
is most vulnerable

00:20:21.320 --> 00:20:26.570
when it's in transit, and
for this reason securing

00:20:26.570 --> 00:20:30.500
data in transit is our
highest priority at Google.

00:20:30.500 --> 00:20:33.660
Data traveling between customers
devices and our data centers

00:20:33.660 --> 00:20:39.420
is encrypted using HTTPS/TLS,
so Transport Layer Security.

00:20:39.420 --> 00:20:43.590
And in fact, Google was the
first major cloud provider

00:20:43.590 --> 00:20:47.820
to enable TLS encryption.

00:20:47.820 --> 00:20:50.060
And this homogeneous
environment is

00:20:50.060 --> 00:20:53.390
maintained by proprietary
software that continually

00:20:53.390 --> 00:20:57.920
monitors the systems binaries
for any binary modifications.

00:21:01.180 --> 00:21:03.310
For our teams to
be successful, they

00:21:03.310 --> 00:21:06.880
must have advanced
real time visibility

00:21:06.880 --> 00:21:08.560
into the status
and functionality

00:21:08.560 --> 00:21:10.502
of our infrastructure.

00:21:10.502 --> 00:21:13.200
As you know, to say that
Google is obsessed with data

00:21:13.200 --> 00:21:15.640
might be a little bit
of an understatement.

00:21:15.640 --> 00:21:21.700
So our teams build systems that
allow us to monitor everything.

00:21:21.700 --> 00:21:24.270
We've built world class
monitoring and control systems

00:21:24.270 --> 00:21:25.800
for all functional areas--

00:21:25.800 --> 00:21:28.680
from the servers, and
storage, and network,

00:21:28.680 --> 00:21:31.990
to the electrical distribution,
the mechanical cooling systems,

00:21:31.990 --> 00:21:33.630
and the security systems.

00:21:33.630 --> 00:21:37.860
We're monitoring all aspects
of performance and operations

00:21:37.860 --> 00:21:39.210
from chip to chiller.

00:21:43.670 --> 00:21:46.070
And as I've mentioned,
energy efficiency

00:21:46.070 --> 00:21:48.360
is at the heart of our
sustainability program.

00:21:48.360 --> 00:21:51.800
We believe that the best
energy saved is the energy not

00:21:51.800 --> 00:21:54.480
consumed in the first place.

00:21:54.480 --> 00:21:56.960
That's why we pioneered
hyperefficient computing

00:21:56.960 --> 00:21:59.540
systems and data centers.

00:21:59.540 --> 00:22:03.380
And that's why our data centers
consume, on average, only half

00:22:03.380 --> 00:22:06.300
of the energy of a typical
enterprise data center.

00:22:09.990 --> 00:22:12.690
What makes us really
stand out in this regard,

00:22:12.690 --> 00:22:14.730
is how we've applied
machine learning

00:22:14.730 --> 00:22:17.100
to drive energy
efficiency even further.

00:22:17.100 --> 00:22:19.890
If you've been in
the keynote speeches

00:22:19.890 --> 00:22:22.350
over the last couple
of days, you've

00:22:22.350 --> 00:22:26.400
heard how much emphasis Google
has been placing on our machine

00:22:26.400 --> 00:22:27.960
learning programs.

00:22:27.960 --> 00:22:30.690
Well, you can imagine
our data centers are

00:22:30.690 --> 00:22:33.480
very large and complex,
where the electrical

00:22:33.480 --> 00:22:36.660
and mechanical control systems
are all working together

00:22:36.660 --> 00:22:39.450
to deliver optimal performance.

00:22:39.450 --> 00:22:41.690
But because of the sheer
number of interactions

00:22:41.690 --> 00:22:45.090
and possible settings
for all of these systems,

00:22:45.090 --> 00:22:47.540
it's impossible
for us mere mortals

00:22:47.540 --> 00:22:50.780
to visualize what the
best ways to set up

00:22:50.780 --> 00:22:54.650
the data center controls are
for those specific operating

00:22:54.650 --> 00:22:58.200
conditions, and to
do it in real time.

00:22:58.200 --> 00:23:00.650
However, really, it's fairly
trivial for the computers

00:23:00.650 --> 00:23:04.160
to crunch through all of
these possible scenarios

00:23:04.160 --> 00:23:07.170
and find the optimal settings.

00:23:07.170 --> 00:23:08.650
So over the past
couple of years,

00:23:08.650 --> 00:23:10.620
we've developed an
algorithm, and we've

00:23:10.620 --> 00:23:12.450
trained it with
billions of data points

00:23:12.450 --> 00:23:15.240
from all of our data
centers all over the world.

00:23:15.240 --> 00:23:17.490
We now use machine
learning models

00:23:17.490 --> 00:23:21.240
to help visualize the status so
the operations teams can set up

00:23:21.240 --> 00:23:23.820
the data center electrical
and cooling plants

00:23:23.820 --> 00:23:28.400
for the most optimal efficient
performance on any given day.

00:23:28.400 --> 00:23:32.060
It's also used considering up
to 19 independent variables

00:23:32.060 --> 00:23:35.840
like the weather outside, the
temperature, the humidity,

00:23:35.840 --> 00:23:37.970
the wind direction,
the wind speed.

00:23:37.970 --> 00:23:42.230
All of these things go into how
efficient a data center runs.

00:23:42.230 --> 00:23:44.240
But without a machine
learning model

00:23:44.240 --> 00:23:46.700
it's almost impossible
for the operations teams

00:23:46.700 --> 00:23:51.190
to try and adjust
that in real time.

00:23:51.190 --> 00:23:54.280
So, in partnership with
our colleagues at DeepMind,

00:23:54.280 --> 00:23:56.200
our data center
team built models

00:23:56.200 --> 00:23:59.200
to recognize and learn
from these patterns

00:23:59.200 --> 00:24:01.180
in the large amounts
of operational data

00:24:01.180 --> 00:24:02.890
that we collect
every day anyhow.

00:24:02.890 --> 00:24:07.000
We just were storing it and
not really using it that well.

00:24:07.000 --> 00:24:11.980
So now, over some trial and
error and perfecting the model,

00:24:11.980 --> 00:24:16.830
the model is now over 99.6%
accurate in predicting our PUE.

00:24:16.830 --> 00:24:19.600
And PUE is Power
Usage Effectiveness,

00:24:19.600 --> 00:24:23.100
it's a measure of data
center efficiency.

00:24:23.100 --> 00:24:31.030
And it has helped us to identify
up to 40% savings in the data

00:24:31.030 --> 00:24:32.580
center cooling overhead.

00:24:32.580 --> 00:24:38.280
This equates to an overall
15% reduction in our PUE,

00:24:38.280 --> 00:24:40.530
which may not seem
like a lot, but if you

00:24:40.530 --> 00:24:43.680
think about the hundreds
of megawatts of data center

00:24:43.680 --> 00:24:46.020
capacity that we have
online every year,

00:24:46.020 --> 00:24:49.290
that is equated to several
tens of millions of dollars

00:24:49.290 --> 00:24:51.180
in energy savings per year.

00:24:54.420 --> 00:24:59.170
But you might wonder,
why do I care?

00:24:59.170 --> 00:25:01.440
Why do I care if your data
centers are efficient?

00:25:01.440 --> 00:25:03.660
Well, one of the
reasons why you care

00:25:03.660 --> 00:25:08.070
is because our operating
costs are directly related

00:25:08.070 --> 00:25:10.290
to the efficiency
of our data centers.

00:25:10.290 --> 00:25:13.410
Our pricing on Google
Cloud is directly related

00:25:13.410 --> 00:25:15.010
to our operating costs.

00:25:15.010 --> 00:25:17.100
So the more efficient
we are internally,

00:25:17.100 --> 00:25:19.560
the more of that savings
we can pass through to you,

00:25:19.560 --> 00:25:22.650
and we can offer the most high
performance lowest costs public

00:25:22.650 --> 00:25:23.887
cloud available.

00:25:28.422 --> 00:25:30.630
And leveraging our strength
in this machine learning,

00:25:30.630 --> 00:25:34.590
we've succeeded in increasing
the overall efficiency

00:25:34.590 --> 00:25:36.660
of our data center fleet.

00:25:36.660 --> 00:25:39.690
This makes Google the
data center you need,

00:25:39.690 --> 00:25:42.720
the one that you really
can't afford to be without--

00:25:42.720 --> 00:25:45.750
high performance computing,
available around the world,

00:25:45.750 --> 00:25:50.460
secure, reliable, where you
need it, when you need it

00:25:50.460 --> 00:25:52.650
and how you need it.

00:25:52.650 --> 00:25:57.800
[MUSIC PLAYING]

